In case \upsilon is infinite, K_\upsilon is one of the complete Archimedean fields {\bf R} or ; and if is a finite place, K is a finite complete extension of some field Qp of p-adic numbers. The \textit {adele ring} AK of K is then the locally compact topological ring given by the restricted direct product
\begin{align*}
\mathbb {A}_K = \prod _{\upsilon \mid \infty } K_\upsilon \times \mathop {\mmlmultiscripts{\prod {\mmlnone }{\prime }}}\limits _{\upsilon < \infty } K_\upsilon ,
\end{align*}
which conveniently bundles together all possible completions of the number field K. We recall that the restricted direct product defining AK is characterized by the fact that, as far as the coordinates belonging to the finite places are concerned, at most finitely many coordinates lie outside of the ring O:= OK of integers in K. A particularly pleasant property of \mathbb {A}_K is that the number field K embeds diagonally into it. Indeed, if x \in K^\times is any element with minimal polynomial f^+ \in {\bf Q}\mathopen {}\mathclose {\left[X \right]}, and f^- \in {\bf Q}\mathopen {}\mathclose {\left[X \right]} is the minimal polynomial of 1/x, then for every prime rational p that doesn't divide any of the coefficients of f^+ or f^-, one has f^+, \, f^- \in {\bf Z}_p \mathopen {}\mathclose {\left[X \right]}. That is, both x and 1/x are integral over {\bf Z}_p. Hence, for any finite place \upsilon lying above p, \mathopen {}\mathclose {\left| x \right|}_\upsilon ^{\pm 1} \leqslant 1, which forces \mathopen {}\mathclose {\left| x \right|}_\upsilon = 1.

